# Cursor Rule: Markdown Auto-Formatting

## Description

Automatically enforce consistent, readable, and maintainable Markdown formatting across the project.

## Rules

Use standard Markdown format, following markdownlint rules.

### Document Structure

- Use only one top-level `#` heading per document at the beginning
- Ensure heading hierarchy doesn't skip levels (e.g., `##` should not be followed by `####`)
- Add blank lines before and after all headings
- Use title case for main headings (## and above)
- Use sentence case for subheadings (### and below)

### Headings

- Ensure exactly one space after `#` markers
- Remove trailing `#` markers from headings
- Use ATX-style headings (`#`) instead of setext-style (`===` or `---`)
- Maintain consistent heading ID format when needed: `{#heading-id}`

### Lists

- Convert all unordered list markers (`*` or `+`) to `-`
- Use `1.` for ordered lists with auto-numbering
- Indent nested lists with exactly 2 spaces per level
- Add blank line before and after list blocks
- Use consistent spacing in list items (one space after marker)
- For task lists, use `- [ ]` for unchecked and `- [x]` for checked items

### Code and Syntax

- Wrap multi-line code in fenced code blocks with language identifiers
- Use single backticks for inline code: `code`
- Add correct language identifier: ` ```python`, ` ```bash`, ` ```json`, etc.
- Always specify a language for fenced code blocks (use `text` for plain text)
- Ensure blank lines before and after code blocks
- Preserve original indentation within code blocks
- Use triple backticks (```` ``` ````) instead of indented code blocks for multi-line code

### Links and References

- Use `[text](url)` format for all links
- Convert reference-style links to inline format when appropriate
- Prefer relative paths for internal project links
- Ensure link text is descriptive and meaningful
- Format email links as `[email@example.com](mailto:email@example.com)`
- Use angle brackets for bare URLs: `<https://example.com>`

### Images

- Use `![alt text](path/to/image.png)` format
- Provide meaningful alt text for all images
- Use relative paths when referencing project images
- Add title attributes when helpful: `![alt](image.png "Title")`
- Place images in appropriate subdirectories (prefer `images/` or `assets/`)

### Tables

- Use pipe-separated format with proper alignment
- Include header separator row with appropriate alignment: `|---|`, `|:---|`, `|---:|`, `|:---:|`
- Align pipes vertically for readability
- Add blank lines before and after tables
- Keep table content concise and readable
- Use header row for all tables

### Text Formatting

- Use `**bold**` instead of `__bold__`
- Use `*italic*` instead of `_italic_`
- Avoid mixing emphasis styles within the same document
- Use `***bold italic***` for combined emphasis
- Avoid unnecessary nested emphasis

### Blockquotes

- Use `>` with exactly one space after for blockquotes
- Nest blockquotes with `>>` for multiple levels
- Add blank lines before and after blockquote blocks
- Use proper attribution when quoting: `> Quote text — Author`

### Horizontal Rules

- Use `---` (three hyphens) for horizontal rules
- Add blank lines before and after horizontal rules
- Avoid using `***` or `___` for horizontal rules

### Whitespace and Line Breaks

- Use Unix line endings (LF)
- Remove trailing whitespace from all lines
- End files with a single newline character
- Use blank lines to separate content blocks
- Limit line length to 80 characters (except for code, tables, and long URLs)
- Use two trailing spaces for hard line breaks when necessary

### Special Characters

- Escape special markdown characters when used literally: `\*`, `\_`, `\#`, etc.
- Use HTML entities for special characters when needed: `&nbsp;`, `&lt;`, `&gt;`
- Use proper quotation marks: `"smart quotes"` instead of `"straight quotes"`

### Footnotes

- Use numbered footnotes: `[^1]`, `[^2]`, etc.
- Place footnote definitions at the end of the document
- Format footnote definitions: `[^1]: Footnote content here.`

### File Organization

- Use lowercase filenames with hyphens: `my-document.md`
- Include meaningful front matter when applicable
- Organize content with consistent heading structure
- Use descriptive filenames that reflect content

### Quality Standards

- Remove empty sections and redundant content
- Ensure all links are functional and up-to-date
- Use consistent terminology throughout
- Spell-check all content
- Replace raw HTML with Markdown equivalents where possible
- Maintain consistent voice and tone

## Intent

Cursor should automatically format Markdown documents to enforce consistency, readability, and maintainability without requiring manual intervention, while preserving semantic meaning and content structure.
